{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he saw the first portion for himself.</b> Meaning, the first portion — which is the Torah — was fitting for him. And the reason for the matter is ״כִּי שָׁם חֶלְקַת״ — meaning, the portion of the lawgiver, who is Moses, ״סָפוּן״ (hidden), or a portion from the lawgiver, for the greater portion ascended on high, and nothing remained there but his holy living body, hidden. And the reason for the matter in bringing ״רָאשֵׁי עָם״ (heads of the people) is as they said, of blessed memory (Sifrei here), to bring the wilderness generation into the World to Come. And this is what it says ״וַיֵּתֵא״ etc., calling them ״רָאשֵׁי עָם״ because they were the beginning of Israel's acquisition for HaShem as a treasured people." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that ״וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית״ — the inheritance that Israel inherited, which is the land of Sihon and Og — ״לוֹ״, meaning to take it as his portion. And out of concern for the precious quality of the land, that everyone who dwells in it or everyone buried in it has a portion in the World to Come (Ketubot 111a), it states the reason that he did not concern himself with this: ״כִּי שָׁם״ — meaning, in that place which he took for his inheritance, there ״חֶלְקַת מְחֹקֵק״, who is Moses, ״סָפוּן״, ״וַיֵּתֵא רָאשֵׁי עָם״, and from now on he too will come with the heads of the people into the World to Come." "<b>The righteousness of HaShem he performed.</b> Meaning, this turns back to Moses, giving the reason why he merited all this distinction, that he brings others with him into the World to Come. And it says ״צִדְקַת ה׳״ etc. — meaning that he performed justice in a matter pertaining to HaShem." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that it intends to say that he did more than his obligation toward HaShem, as we alluded to in the verse ״וּצְדָקָה תִהְיֶה לָּנוּ״ etc. (Deuteronomy 6:25). Also His judgments with Israel he performed — meaning, he would constantly plead with HaShem on behalf of Israel and would tip the scales toward merit, as stated in the matter of the Calf and the Spies and the congregation of Korah; see what we explained concerning them. According to this, the measure of what it says is ״וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו״ — meaning, the judgments of HaShem with Israel, ״עָשָׂה״ them — meaning, he performed, he set them right." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this line: ״צִדְקַת ה׳ עָשָׂה״ — in what pertained to himself; and that which he himself was judged with these judgments to die across the Jordan — and he closed his prayer — is ״עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל״, meaning, it was in the things that were with Israel, such as the waters of Meribah and the rest of the causes that came from them." "<b>Or</b> it may be interpreted that his saying ״צִדְקַת ה׳״ etc. refers to Gad, who did with HaShem a thing that is reckoned to him as righteousness — namely, that he crossed the Jordan to wage the obligatory war together with Israel. And this thing, as regards the law between him and HaShem, he is not obligated in, for their inheritance came in the place they chose, and they have no obligation to cross over to wage war against the inhabitants of the land, as they argued in their arguments in parashat Matot (Numbers 32:5), and as we explained there, and as the fact proved — that HaShem agreed to their inheritance, for it had already come about there. And their crossing the Jordan is reckoned to them as righteousness, this being with respect to what concerns them and HaShem; but as for what concerns them and Israel, strict justice applies." "<b>And this</b> is his saying ״וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and as I explained there in Moses' reply to their arguments, that all of it was so they would be clear from HaShem and from Israel, for HaShem has no need for the aid of their might. And HaShem knew that their intent in asking for the land was not from any evil motive, and all the arguments that Moses argued against them were only in relation to Israel their brothers."], :book :deuteronomy, 